Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they help us answer the question  

• `event_date` : restrict the analysis to December 2020 (`'20201201'‑'20201231'`).  
• `event_name` : recognise page views (`'page_view'`) and purchases (`'purchase'`).  
• `user_pseudo_id` : stable user identifier to count events per user.  
• `ecommerce` / `items` (or simply `event_name='purchase'`) : decide whether a user is a “purchaser”.  
• `event_params` : holds extra information (page title, page_location, etc.) but not strictly needed here.  

Conditions that will be useful  

• `event_date BETWEEN '20201201' AND '20201231'`  
• `event_name = 'page_view'`   → counts of pageviews  
• `event_name = 'purchase'`   → flags purchasers  
• Wild‑card table use: `\`bigquery‑public‑data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*\`` to cover every 202012XX daily table in one query.  

Below are 10 exploratory → analytic SQL statements (all different).  
Each finishes with `LIMIT 20` so you can run them safely.  

```sql
-- 1. Distinct GA4 daily tables we will touch (sanity‑check the suffixes)
SELECT
  _TABLE_SUFFIX AS day_suffix
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
GROUP BY
  day_suffix
ORDER BY
  day_suffix
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. What event names occur in December 2020?
SELECT DISTINCT
  event_name
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
ORDER BY
  event_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. How many purchase events did each December day have?
SELECT
  _TABLE_SUFFIX AS event_day,
  COUNT(*) AS purchase_events
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
WHERE
  event_name = 'purchase'
GROUP BY
  event_day
ORDER BY
  event_day
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Peek at the ecommerce record of some purchase events
SELECT
  event_date,
  user_pseudo_id,
  ecommerce
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
WHERE
  event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Look at a few page_view events and their parameters
SELECT
  event_date,
  user_pseudo_id,
  event_params
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
WHERE
  event_name = 'page_view'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Count pageviews per user in December (sample 20 rows only)
SELECT
  user_pseudo_id,
  COUNTIF(event_name = 'page_view') AS pageviews_dec2020
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
GROUP BY
  user_pseudo_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Flag whether each user made at least one purchase in December
SELECT
  user_pseudo_id,
  MAX(CASE WHEN event_name = 'purchase' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS is_purchaser
FROM
  `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
GROUP BY
  user_pseudo_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Combine pageview counts with purchaser flag (sample 20)
WITH per_user AS (
  SELECT
    user_pseudo_id,
    COUNTIF(event_name = 'page_view') AS pageviews,
    MAX(CASE WHEN event_name = 'purchase' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS is_purchaser
  FROM
    `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
  GROUP BY
    user_pseudo_id
)
SELECT * FROM per_user
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Average December pageviews for purchasers vs non‑purchasers
WITH per_user AS (
  SELECT
    user_pseudo_id,
    COUNTIF(event_name = 'page_view') AS pageviews,
    MAX(CASE WHEN event_name = 'purchase' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS is_purchaser
  FROM
    `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
  GROUP BY
    user_pseudo_id
)
SELECT
  is_purchaser,
  AVG(pageviews) AS avg_pageviews
FROM
  per_user
GROUP BY
  is_purchaser
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: average difference in pageviews (purchasers ‑ non‑purchasers)
WITH per_user AS (
  SELECT
    user_pseudo_id,
    COUNTIF(event_name = 'page_view') AS pageviews,
    MAX(CASE WHEN event_name = 'purchase' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS is_purchaser
  FROM
    `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202012*`
  GROUP BY
    user_pseudo_id
),
aggregates AS (
  SELECT
    AVG(IF(is_purchaser = 1, pageviews, NULL)) AS avg_purchaser,
    AVG(IF(is_purchaser = 0, pageviews, NULL)) AS avg_non_purchaser
  FROM
    per_user
)
SELECT
  avg_purchaser - avg_non_purchaser AS avg_pageview_diff
FROM
  aggregates
LIMIT 20;
```

